\section{What the columns mean}{


\code{obs_sd} and \code{pred_sd} are the SD of \strong{one observation across subjects} --
not between-subject variability. They carry BSV, residual error, and,
wherever a study marginalises a covariate, the spread that covariate
induces. \code{struct_sd} is the same quantity \strong{before} residual error is
composed on, so \code{pred_sd - struct_sd} is what sigma contributes: a predicted
spread that misses the observed one can then be attributed. \code{struct_sd} is
\code{NA} for a transforming error model, which moves the mean as well and leaves
the pre-sigma variance on a different scale.

\code{z} is the mean standardised residual, \code{(obs - pred) / sqrt(pred_var / n)}.
}

\section{Sources and strata}{


\code{stratify} bands a source into one study per covariate level, because a
covariate a study marginalises over is not identified against a random
effect on the same parameter. Those strata are the unit of the likelihood,
not a unit a reader recognises, so \code{by = "source"} (the default) puts them
back together: the mean is the n-weighted mean, and the variance is the law
of total variance -- within plus \strong{between}, the between term being the
covariate effect the banding created.

\code{by = "stratum"} returns them separately, which is what you want when the
between-level contrast is the thing you are looking at.
}

